:synohw_overview_plug:
> **The drawing** is made from the same live values as the tables: each bay shows the disk Unraid reports there (temperature, or standby / no reading), coloured by the band of the fan table that drives that unit (main unit: the running DS2415+ table; expansion unit: DSM's DX1215 table) and labelled in text; Tab moves through the populated bays, each with its full description. Fans turn at a rate taken from the **commanded** level (DS2415+: duty sent, 20 % = one turn in 2.4 s, 99 % = 0.48 s; DX1215: low 2 s, middle 1.2 s, full 0.6 s); neither unit reports an RPM. A fan with its alarm bit set is drawn stopped and red. The network wires and the expansion cable move while there is a link. With "reduce motion" set in the operating system, all of it moves 4 times slower instead of stopping.
>
> **Only the DS2415+ and the DX1215 are drawn.** Any other unit gets a plain picture with its name and the same values in the cards and tables. The main unit is recognised by its PCI controllers (no model string is read); the expansion unit by the model and unique ID that the synology_dx module reads from it.
>
> **Bay numbers are confirmed** at the box by blink count (2026-10-26, `experiments/2026-10-26-bay-map/RESULT.md`). Main unit: bay = 4 x controller index (88SE9215 03:00.0, 05:00.0, 06:00.0) + port + 1, as DSM's disk index map says (`DiskIdxMap=1b1f0004080c`); the Seagate on 03:00.0 port 0 is in bay 1. DX1215: bay = 3 x EMID + port multiplier port + 1; the WD on the EMID 2 port multiplier, port 1, is in bay 8.
>
> **Tray LEDs** show what the synology_dx driver has set: green = presence/activity (the controllers drive it once the LEDs are on), orange = fault from disk health (this plugin's daemon), an empty ring = off. Details and the table per bay: DS2415+ and DX1215 tabs, Bay LEDs.
:end

